From 9ec5a9428439b3990c67e40f55de9a44fba75929 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Romain Dolbeau <romain@dolbeau.org>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 09:33:21 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] hw/scsi/sun3-si, hw/m68k/sun3: carry the Verilated core
 instead

The board grows core=auto|sw|rtl and the machine grows si-core= and
si-rtl= to reach it.  Everything that makes this a Sun-3 board rather
than a 5380 - the Am9516 chain parsing, the packing FIFO, the CSR
semantics, the "the OBIO si ignores fifo_count during a DATA phase"
rule, the VME register shadows, and the three status-bit decisions
SunOS settled - is untouched.  Eight one-line dispatchers are the whole
difference, because all of that logic is about the chip's observable
pins and not about how the chip is built.

The board is still created unconditionally; si-core= and si-rtl= only
say which 5380 goes behind the first eight registers.

Two things arrive with the RTL path and belong only to it.

The pacing.  A Verilated core advances when it is stepped and not
otherwise, and the SCSI target and SD card behind it have to advance
while the guest is between accesses - so there is a catch-up to virtual
time, bounded so a long gap cannot cost an unbounded simulation, a
microsecond bought by each register access, and a 500 us timer to make
progress when the guest is doing something else entirely.  None of it
is created for the software chip, which has no time in it.

And the pump waits again.  With the software chip it moves what it can
and returns, because there is nothing to spin for and spinning inside
an MMIO handler would deadlock the AIO path it is waiting on.  A
Verilated core is the other way round: DRQ only comes up if something
steps it, so the only way to see the next byte is to step it and look.
The wait is bounded by DRQ_WAIT_NS and resets on every byte, so the
budget is "how long a gap between bytes is still a live transfer" - and
the longest real gap is the SD card fetching the next block.
sun3_si_settle is the same idea at the end of a transfer: the chip
raises its interrupt a little after the last byte, and a line that is
only reconsidered on the next 500 us tick is an interrupt a hundred
transfer-lengths late, which is what a driver calls lost.

This is not upstream material and is not offered as such: it dlopens a
library built from another project's RTL.

Verified: the 3/60 PROM reads a Sun label and loads a kernel on both
cores; NetBSD 10.1 attaches si0 and mounts root on both; SunOS 4.1.1
boots on both, and cosim/scripts/diff-sun3-dma.py drives the two
through the same DMA transfers and compares every read.
